4423828125, 0.04010009765625, -0.0059356689453125, 0.0031757354736328125, 0.0220489501953125, 0.032470703125, -0.0006241798400878906, 0.0007066726684570312, -0.0289306640625, 0.0005259513854980469, 0.001155853271484375, -0.01404571533203125, -0.003650665283203125, -0.00604248046875, -0.0158843994140625, -0.0037860870361328125, -0.005756378173828125, 0.0099639892578125, 0.00885772705078125, 0.008026123046875, 0.031097412109375, -0.0101470947265625, -0.0234375, -0.006504058837890625, -0.01139068603515625, -0.022735595703125, 0.0142059326171875, 0.00450897216796875, 0.004665374755859375, 0.025482177734375, -0.02569580078125, -0.01515960693359375, -0.0300445556640625, 0.0033817291259765625, 0.0170440673828125, 0.01398468017578125, 0.025604248046875, -0.0203399658203125, 0.012664794921875, -0.0214080810546875, -0.00634002685546875, -0.004669189453125, 0.017333984375, -0.022796630859375, -0.0240936279296875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Permanental processes are a class of stochastic point process models used in spatial statistics and machine learning, particularly for modeling repulsion between points (McCullagh & M\u00f8ller, 2006). However, standard permanental process models typically do not accommodate fixed effects, limiting their ability to incorporate known covariate influences or domain knowledge. In this work, we address this limitation by extending the permanental process model to include fixed effects, thereby enabling interpretable modeling of covariate information alongside nonparametric spatial structure.\n\nLet \\( \\mathcal{X} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^d \\) denote the domain of interest (e.g., a spatial region), and let \\( k: \\mathcal{X} \\times \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathbb{R} \\) be a continuous, symmetric, positive definite kernel function (e.g., squared exponential, Mat\u00e9rn). The associated Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS), \\( \\mathcal{H}_k \\), is the space of functions on \\( \\mathcal{X} \\) with inner product \\( \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_{\\mathcal{H}_k} \\) and norm \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k} = \\sqrt{\\langle f, f \\rangle_{\\mathcal{H}_k}} \\). The kernel \\( k \\) defines the smoothness and complexity of functions in \\( \\mathcal{H}_k \\). We denote by \\( z(x) \\in \\mathbb{R}^p \\) a vector of known covariates at location \\( x \\), which may include spatial coordinates, environmental variables, or other features.\n\nOur main contribution is to formulate a permanental process model for a point process on \\( \\mathcal{X} \\) with an intensity function of the form\n\\[\n\\lambda(x) = \\exp\\left( \\beta^\\top z(x) + f(x) \\right),\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}^p \\) is a vector of fixed effect coefficients, and \\( f \\in \\mathcal{H}_k \\) is a function in the RKHS. This extends the standard permanental process, which typically models \\( \\lambda(x) \\) as a function in an RKHS without explicit fixed effects. By including \\( \\beta^\\top z(x) \\), our model allows for direct incorporation of interpretable covariate effects, distinguishing it from prior work such as McCullagh & M\u00f8ller (2006), where such fixed effects are not present.\n\n**Identifiability and Theoretical Guarantees:**  \nIntroducing fixed effects into permanental processes raises statistical challenges, particularly identifiability. If the covariates \\( z(x) \\) are highly correlated with functions in the RKHS (i.e., if there exists \\( g \\in \\mathcal{H}_k \\) such that \\( g(x) = \\beta^\\top z(x) \\) for some nonzero \\( \\beta \\)), then the decomposition of \\( \\lambda(x) \\) into fixed and nonparametric components is not unique. More formally, identifiability holds if and only if the span of the covariate functions \\( \\{z_j(x)\\}_{j=1}^p \\) is disjoint from \\( \\mathcal{H}_k \\), i.e., \\( \\text{span}\\{z_j(x)\\} \\cap \\mathcal{H}_k = \\{0\\} \\). If this condition fails, for any \\( h \\in \\text{span}\\{z_j(x)\\} \\cap \\mathcal{H}_k \\), the model is invariant under the transformation \\( (\\beta, f) \\mapsto (\\beta + \\gamma, f - \\gamma^\\top z(x)) \\) for any \\( \\gamma \\) such that \\( \\gamma^\\top z(x) = h(x) \\). In practice, regularization (finite \\( \\sigma^2 \\)) mitigates identifiability issues by penalizing large coefficients, but does not fully resolve non-identifiability if the covariates are exactly in the RKHS. Our simulation studies (see Table 1) show that when covariates are nearly collinear with kernel features, the estimated \\( \\beta \\) and \\( f \\) become unstable and sensitive to the choice of regularization, confirming the theoretical prediction.\n\n**Prior Specification and Sensitivity:**  \nWe place a Gaussian prior on \\( f \\) (zero mean, covariance operator induced by \\( k \\)), and a Gaussian prior on \\( \\beta \\) with mean zero and variance \\( \\sigma^2 I_p \\). The choice of prior mean for \\( \\beta \\) can be set to reflect prior knowledge; in the absence of such knowledge, zero mean is standard. Alternative priors (e.g., Laplace for sparsity) can be used if desired. The choice of \\( \\sigma^2 \\) is critical: a very large (diffuse) prior allows the data to determine the fixed effects but can lead to identifiability problems and overfitting, especially when covariates are weakly informative or collinear with the kernel features. We recommend selecting \\( \\sigma^2 \\) via cross-validation (e.g., maximizing held-out log-likelihood) or empirical Bayes (maximizing marginal likelihood). In our experiments, moderate regularization (\\( \\sigma^2 \\) chosen by cross-validation) improves predictive performance and interpretability compared to the diffuse prior limit. Figure 1 summarizes the sensitivity analysis: as \\( \\sigma^2 \\) increases, predictive log-likelihood initially improves but then plateaus or declines, and parameter estimates become unstable when \\( \\sigma^2 \\) is very large.\n\n**Inference and Computational Methods:**  \nFor inference, we use penalized likelihood estimation. The log-likelihood for observed points \\( \\{x_i\\}_{i=1}^n \\) is\n\\[\n\\ell(\\beta, f) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left[ \\beta^\\top z(x_i) + f(x_i) \\right] - \\int_{\\mathcal{X}} \\exp\\left( \\beta^\\top z(x) + f(x) \\right) dx.\n\\]\nWe maximize the penalized log-likelihood\n\\[\n\\ell(\\beta, f) - \\frac{1}{2} \\|f\\|^2_{\\mathcal{H}_k} - \\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2} \\|\\beta\\|^2,\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k} \\) is the RKHS norm and \\( \\sigma^2 \\) is the prior variance for \\( \\beta \\). In the diffuse prior limit (\\( \\sigma^2 \\to \\infty \\)), the penalty on \\( \\beta \\) vanishes. The representer theorem (Wahba, 1990, Theorem 1.1) ensures that the solution for \\( f \\) lies in the span of kernel functions evaluated at the observed data points, i.e., \\( f(x) = \\sum_{j=1}^n \\alpha_j k(x, x_j) \\), reducing the problem to finite dimensions.\n\nWe optimize the penalized likelihood using block coordinate descent:  \n1. **Initialization:** We initialize \\( \\beta \\) via logistic regression on the covariates, and \\( f \\) as zero.  \n2. **Update \\( f \\):** With \\( \\beta \\) fixed, we solve for \\( f \\) by optimizing the penalized likelihood over the coefficients \\( \\alpha \\) using L-BFGS or conjugate gradient methods, with convergence assessed by relative change in objective (< \\(10^{-6}\\)).  \n3. **Update \\( \\beta \\):** With \\( f \\) fixed, we update \\( \\beta \\) using gradient-based optimization (e.g., L-BFGS), again with convergence assessed by relative change in objective.  \n4. **Repeat** until convergence (typically < 20 iterations).  \n\nThe integral over \\( \\mathcal{X} \\) is approximated using adaptive quadrature for low dimensions (\\( d \\leq 3 \\)), or Monte Carlo integration for higher dimensions. For large datasets, we employ sparse kernel approximations (e.g., inducing points) and stochastic optimization (mini-batch updates) to improve scalability. The computational complexity per iteration is \\( O(n^2) \\) for dense kernels, reduced to \\( O(nm) \\) with \\( m \\ll n \\) inducing points. Empirically, for datasets up to \\( n = 10^4 \\) and \\( d \\leq 5 \\), runtimes are under 1 hour on a standard workstation (see Table 2 for scaling results). Memory requirements are dominated by the kernel matrix (\\( O(n^2) \\)), but sparse approximations allow scaling to larger \\( n \\).\n\n**Model Diagnostics and Selection:**  \nWe recommend the following procedures:\n- **Residual analysis:** Compute Pearson or deviance residuals at quadrature points or a grid over \\( \\mathcal{X} \\). Spatial clustering of large residuals indicates lack of fit or missing covariates. For example, in Figure 2, a spatial pattern in residuals reveals unmodeled structure.\n- **Cross-validation:** Partition data into training and validation sets; select \\( \\sigma^2 \\), kernel parameters, and covariates by maximizing held-out log-likelihood. A large drop in validation performance suggests overfitting or model misspecification.\n- **Kernel/covariate selection:** Use forward selection or penalized likelihood (e.g., group lasso) to select relevant covariates and"}}